Hepatitis A
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Abrupt fever/anorexia, Dark urine/jaundice, History of travel/contaminated food, Positive anti-HAV IgM

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-032
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of severe, exhausting fatigue, high fevers, and the sudden appearance of deeply yellowed eyes and dark urine following recent international travel.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an abrupt onset of severe systemic illness with new-onset jaundice and dark urine. Urgent out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was necessary to evaluate for acute fulminant liver failure (encephalopathy, bleeding), rule out ascending cholangitis, and determine if the patient was stable enough for outpatient management while pending urgent serological confirmation prior to the next available routine consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I came back from Bali a few weeks ago, and suddenly I feel like I've been hit by a truck. I can't look at food, and my urine looks like black coffee."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an abrupt onset of low-grade fever, severe fatigue, and profound systemic malaise starting 4 days ago. This prodrome was followed by profound anorexia, nausea, and recurrent vomiting. Over the past 48 hours, noted right upper quadrant discomfort, intensely dark urine, and pale, acholic stools. This morning, patient noticed scleral icterus and generalized jaundice. Reports intense generalized pruritus. Notably reports a sudden, strong aversion to smoking (previously smoked a pack a day). History of recent travel to an endemic area (Bali) 4 weeks prior, admitting to consumption of local street food and tap water.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe fatigue; able to ambulate to the bathroom independently but requires prolonged rest afterward.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; risk of dehydration due to severe anorexia and nausea.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 95 | Temp: 38.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears acutely unwell, visibly jaundiced, lethargic, and frequently scratching their arms.
Physical Exam: Marked scleral icterus and diffuse cutaneous jaundice are present. Abdominal examination reveals right upper quadrant discomfort and a palpable, smooth, tender hepatomegaly extending 2 cm below the costal margin. No splenomegaly. No asterixis or signs of hepatic encephalopathy. No spider naevi or palmar erythema to suggest chronic disease. The clinical picture explicitly demonstrates the diagnostic tetrad: abrupt fever/anorexia, dark urine/jaundice, and a clear history of travel/contaminated food; a positive anti-HAV IgM is strongly suspected pending urgent serology.
Environmental Observations: Several untouched meals in the kitchen; multiple ashtrays are empty.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Hepatitis A - Status: Exacerbated (Acute symptomatic phase)
Differentials:

Acute Cholangitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The more insidious viral prodrome (fatigue, anorexia, smoking aversion) preceding the jaundice, and the absence of the classic Charcot's triad of severe, localized RUQ pain and violent rigors/shaking chills.

Drug-Induced Liver Injury (e.g., Paracetamol toxicity) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a massive paracetamol ingestion history and the presence of a distinct viral prodrome with a strong epidemiological link (recent endemic travel).

Acute Hepatitis B or C - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The abrupt, hyperacute onset of high fever and systemic symptoms, which is highly characteristic of Hepatitis A, whereas HBV and HCV typically have a much more insidious, smoldering presentation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk is moderate due to dehydration potential)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a textbook, hyperacute presentation of viral hepatitis, epidemiologically pointing strongly to Hepatitis A given the recent travel history. The sudden onset of profound jaundice, dark urine, and tender hepatomegaly without signs of ascending cholangitis or biliary colic requires urgent assessment to ensure the liver is not failing entirely (fulminant hepatitis). The physical exam confirms the absence of asterixis (encephalopathy) and gross coagulopathy (bruising), indicating the patient is stable for outpatient supportive care. However, urgent after-hours intervention was necessary to provide parenteral antiemetics, implement strict hydration protocols, and arrange expedited diagnostic serology to formally confirm the highly infectious etiology.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ondansetron 4mg to control nausea. Issued an urgent pathology request for LFTs, Coagulation profile, and a comprehensive viral hepatitis screen (specifically Anti-HAV IgM, HBsAg, Anti-HCV) to be drawn immediately in the morning. Notified the local public health unit of a suspected communicable disease.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the severe itching, take cool, short showers and gently pat your skin dry with a soft towel rather than rubbing aggressively. This physical cooling constricts the superficial blood vessels in your skin, directly reducing the inflammatory signals that are causing the intense, unscratchable itch driven by the high bilirubin levels in your blood.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review in 24-48 hours to urgently review blood results and assess hydration status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient becomes confused, excessively drowsy, develops a flapping tremor in their hands, or begins vomiting uncontrollably, they must present to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a highly contagious viral infection of the liver. Confirmation requires the urgent blood tests provided today.
Clinical Course: The extreme fatigue and jaundice will typically peak over the next 1-2 weeks before slowly resolving over a month. Complete recovery without permanent liver damage is the standard outcome.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must maintain strict hand hygiene, especially after using the toilet. Wash your hands with soap and water for a full 20 seconds to physically destroy the virus and prevent spreading it to anyone else who visits your home.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have likely contracted a virus from contaminated food or water during your trip. The virus is actively attacking your liver cells, causing the organ to swell and fail to process bile. This bile is now spilling into your blood (causing the yellow skin and severe itching) and filtering through your kidneys (causing the dark urine). Your immune system will clear the virus entirely on its own over the coming weeks.
Actionable Steps: Absolute abstinence from all alcohol and paracetamol is mandatory for the next 3 months. Your liver is acutely inflamed and its cellular machinery is compromised; forcing it to process these toxins could easily push it into complete, irreversible failure.

